    faqs: [
      { q: 'Is homeschooling legal in Nigeria?',
        a: 'Yes. The Federal Ministry of Education recognises homeschooling and online learning. Nigerian families using an accredited international provider like Smartious do not need to register separately with state education boards. We issue formal enrolment certificates, termly progress reports and predicted grades that are accepted by Nigerian and international universities.' },
      { q: 'Do you actually support WAEC, or only international curricula?',
        a: 'Both — and this is one of the reasons Nigerian families choose us. We run a dedicated WAEC preparation track covering the full WAEC SSCE syllabus, taught by Nigerian-experienced subject specialists in Mathematics, English Language, Physics, Chemistry, Biology, Economics, Government, Literature, Geography, Christian Religious Studies, Islamic Religious Studies and other WAEC subjects. Students register directly with WAEC as private candidates for either the May/June or November/December series. Many of our Nigerian students do both: WAEC for Nigerian university entry, plus IGCSE or A-Level for international university applications. We help families decide which combination fits their goals.' },
      { q: 'Where do Nigerian students sit their exams?',
        a: 'WAEC examinations are sat at WAEC-designated centres in all 36 states and the FCT. Cambridge IGCSE, Cambridge A-Level and Pearson Edexcel examinations are sat at British Council centres in Lagos, Abuja and Port Harcourt, plus selected international school exam centres accepting private candidates. IB Diploma examinations are sat at authorised IB World Schools in Lagos and Abuja. Smartious handles all registration and provides comprehensive mock examination programmes before each series.' },
      { q: 'How does Smartious cost compare to top Lagos and Abuja international schools?',
        a: 'Premium Lagos schools (AISL, BIS Lagos, Charterhouse Lagos, Rugby School Nigeria) now charge ₦18-35 million per year per child, with application fees of ₦1-2 million on top. Mid-tier international schools charge ₦8-15 million per year. Smartious annual fees for a full programme run roughly USD 3,000-5,000 — at current rates that is approximately ₦4-7 million per year. Same Cambridge, Edexcel, IB and American exam boards, taught live, no application fees, no waitlists.' },
      { q: 'Why do you price in USD instead of Naira?',
        a: 'Because we operate to international standards — our students sit the same Cambridge, Edexcel, IB and American exams as students in London, Dubai, Toronto and Nairobi. USD pricing also protects Nigerian families from school fee inflation: the Naira has lost more than 60% of its USD value since 2022, and many Nigerian schools have raised Naira-denominated fees by 50-100% in the same period. Smartious USD pricing has stayed stable. We accept Naira payments via bank transfer, Paystack, or card at the prevailing exchange rate — you pay in Naira, the price stays predictable in USD terms.' },
      { q: 'Are classes scheduled in Nigerian time?',
        a: 'Yes. Smartious runs live group classes across three time-zone bands — UK & Africa, Americas, and Asia-Pacific — with a stable small team of subject specialists in each band. Nigerian students join the UK & Africa band (WAT is GMT+1, just two hours behind EAT), with live sessions timetabled during Nigerian school hours. Every class is recorded for unlimited rewatch. Students in Lagos, Abuja and across Nigeria join the same live cohorts as classmates in Doha, Nairobi and London.' },
      { q: 'Will Smartious help with applications to UNILAG, Covenant and other Nigerian universities?',
        a: 'Yes. Students completing IGCSE and A-Level through Smartious are eligible to apply to Nigerian universities through JAMB Direct Entry. We provide the transcripts, predicted grades, recommendation letters and counselling support needed for UNILAG (University of Lagos), Covenant University, Babcock, Bowen, Lead City, Pan-Atlantic, Afe Babalola University, ABU Zaria, OAU Ife and others. Students with WAEC results apply through standard JAMB UTME. Many of our Nigerian families end up applying to both Nigerian and international universities to keep options open.' },
      { q: 'What about scholarships and university funding?',
        a: 'Cambridge A-Level and IB Diploma graduates are competitive for: PTDF scholarships (Nigerian government), Commonwealth Scholarships (UK), Chevening (UK), Fulbright (US), DAAD (Germany), and merit scholarships at most US universities. Our UCAS and Common App counselling team helps Year 12-13 students apply for these alongside their university applications. Many international universities also offer their own scholarships specifically for African students with strong A-Level or IB results.' },
      { q: 'Can my child join Smartious mid-term if they are already at a Nigerian school?',
        a: 'Yes. We accept enrolments year-round with no waitlist. Mid-term transfers are routine — your child continues with the same curriculum and exam board without losing progress, whether they are currently at a Nigerian secondary school, a Lagos international school, or moving back from abroad. Most students begin within one week of admissions assessment.' },
      { q: 'Will my child still socialise and make friends learning online?',
        a: 'Yes. Smartious students join live virtual classes daily with classmates across Nigeria and from Kenya, Ghana, the UK, UAE, Canada and beyond. Our Wednesday enrichment programme runs online clubs in coding, debate, Model UN, chess, journalism, AI and leadership. Students build real friendships across the African diaspora — many describe their Smartious classmates as their closest peers, regardless of which Nigerian city they live in.' },
      { q: 'What happens if we want to return to a traditional school?',
        a: 'You can transfer back at any time. Smartious issues full transcripts, termly progress reports and predicted grades that are recognised by Nigerian secondary schools (including the major Lagos, Abuja and Port Harcourt international schools) and by schools internationally. Most schools place returning students based on those records combined with their own placement assessment.' },
      { q: 'Who are the teachers? Are they qualified to teach Nigerian and international curricula?',
        a: 'All Smartious teachers hold degrees in the subjects they teach. Our WAEC track is taught by Nigerian-trained subject specialists with experience preparing students for WASSCE. Our international curriculum teachers hold degrees from Nigerian, Kenyan, UK and international universities, with experience in Cambridge IGCSE, A-Level, IB and American curricula. We screen every candidate through a written subject assessment, a teaching demonstration and a safeguarding check before any contact with students.' },
    ],
