    whyUk: {
      heading: 'Why UK families are choosing online schooling',
      intro: 'Home education in England has more than doubled since 2020. The reasons UK parents tell us they switch are specific, practical, and very different from the ones US or UAE families give.',
      points: [
        { h: 'Independent school fees after the 2025 VAT change', p: 'When the UK government introduced 20% VAT on private school fees in January 2025, independent day-school fees jumped from roughly £18,000 to £22,000+ per year, with senior schools at the top end now well above £40,000. Smartious delivers the same Cambridge and Edexcel programmes for £3,000–£6,000 a year, with the same university outcomes.' },
        { h: 'State school place pressure', p: 'Catchment areas in London, the South East, the West Midlands and many cities are now overstretched. Families who don\'t get their preferred school can wait years for a place, or accept a school an hour\'s commute away. Online schooling sidesteps the catchment lottery entirely.' },
        { h: 'The new CNIS register changes the landscape', p: 'From 2026, mandatory registration with the Local Authority for children not in school is creating a more formal home-education environment. Families want a provider who can supply documented provision reports rather than a DIY approach that may now invite more scrutiny. Smartious provides exactly that documentation.' },
        { h: 'Bullying, anxiety, school refusal', p: 'A growing number of UK families come to us after their child has experienced bullying or anxiety so severe they cannot return to school. Online schooling lets these children continue their education in a calmer environment while exam targets stay on track.' },
        { h: 'Travelling and relocating families', p: 'Families with frequent international moves — diplomatic, military, corporate postings — find traditional schooling deeply disruptive. Smartious travels with the family. Same teachers, same curriculum, same exam board, anywhere in the world.' },
        { h: 'Athletes, performers, gifted learners', p: 'Children with serious sporting commitments, performing-arts careers, or accelerated academic profiles often find a standard school timetable a poor fit. Live classes plus recordings allow training around tournaments, auditions, or competitions without falling behind academically.' },
      ],
    },
